WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:00.951
Welcome.

00:00:00.951 --> 00:00:04.406
This is the Risk Mechanism Theory Index, RMTI,

00:00:04.406 --> 00:00:11.616
created and formulated by Dr. Cesar Marolla to quantify
systemic risk in disaster science and in

00:00:11.616 --> 00:00:12.292
oncology.

00:00:12.292 --> 00:00:18.976
In the next few minutes you will see how one equation
turns a hazard, the people exposed,

00:00:18.976 --> 00:00:24.909
how fragile they are, and how well a system copes, into
a single bounded score.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.099
A reminder before we start.

00:00:28.099 --> 00:00:33.351
RMTI is a research prototype: not validated, not a
medical device,

00:00:33.351 --> 00:00:37.171
and not for patient-care or emergency decisions.

00:00:37.171 --> 00:00:43.379
Everything here uses illustrative values, published
rows, or aggregate counts.

00:00:43.379 --> 00:00:45.289
No patient data appears.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:47.337
Why an index?

00:00:47.337 --> 00:00:54.173
Risk decisions are often made from narrative: the
loudest voice, or the most recent event.

00:00:54.173 --> 00:00:57.136
RMTI moves from narrative to algorithm.

00:00:57.136 --> 00:01:03.060
Four inputs, each between zero and one, each with a
stated meaning and source.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:10.883
And the same fixed thresholds for every patient, every
city and every year, so a score can be compared,

00:01:10.883 --> 00:01:12.630
audited and challenged.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:21.559
The equation multiplies four bounded factors:
likelihood, exposure, vulnerability, and resilience,

00:01:21.559 --> 00:01:23.965
which enters as one minus rho.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.858
L is likelihood: how probable the hazard is in a year.

00:01:28.858 --> 00:01:34.575
In oncology it comes from a validated risk score, such
as aMAP for liver cancer,

00:01:34.575 --> 00:01:36.147
and is not re-derived.

00:01:36.147 --> 00:01:40.577
In disaster science it is the annual probability that a
flood,

00:01:40.577 --> 00:01:43.292
fire or heat event materially strikes.

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:47.383
E is exposure: who or what is in harm's way.

00:01:47.383 --> 00:01:54.138
For liver surveillance it is the share of the at-risk
population inside the programme's footprint.

00:01:54.138 --> 00:01:59.032
For a city it is the share of people and assets inside
the hazard zone.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:04.157
V is vulnerability: how badly those exposed are hurt.

00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:10.969
For the liver it is reserve, taken from the ALBI grade,
which uses albumin and bilirubin.

00:02:10.969 --> 00:02:15.944
For a city it reflects housing, services, poverty and
governance.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:24.067
Multiply L, E and V and you get the inherent risk: the
score if nothing cushioned it.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:30.521
Resilience, rho, is the fraction of that inherent risk
the system absorbs.

00:02:30.521 --> 00:02:36.674
Because it enters as one minus rho, more resilience
always means less residual risk.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:40.103
And that is where action lives.

00:02:40.103 --> 00:02:45.763
Improve surveillance quality, early warning or
infrastructure, raise rho,

00:02:45.763 --> 00:02:48.089
and the residual risk R falls.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:55.024
R lands in one of five fixed tiers: low, moderate, high,
severe and critical.

00:02:55.024 --> 00:02:57.160
Each tier carries a posture.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:03.797
For liver surveillance, it runs from usual-care
monitoring at tier one to an immediate,

00:03:03.797 --> 00:03:06.162
resourced pathway at tier five.

00:03:06.162 --> 00:03:11.960
For cities, from baseline monitoring to an immediate,
system-level response.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.847
Which lever matters most?

00:03:14.847 --> 00:03:21.203
Move each factor by ten points in the risk-reducing
direction and see how far R falls.

00:03:21.203 --> 00:03:26.967
In this example likelihood moves it most, by 0.029,
followed by vulnerability,

00:03:26.967 --> 00:03:28.740
exposure and resilience.

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:35.243
But equal steps are not equal efforts, and likelihood is
often set by nature or biology,

00:03:35.243 --> 00:03:40.564
so programmes mostly act through exposure, vulnerability
and resilience.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:46.445
R measures residual risk, not how bad a miss would be.

00:03:46.445 --> 00:03:55.057
A second layer, expected annual loss, multiplies
likelihood by consequence and by one minus rho.

00:03:55.057 --> 00:03:58.735
Take two liver patients in the same tier.

00:03:58.735 --> 00:04:04.208
Patient A has R of 0.072 and would be caught early and
cured.

00:04:04.208 --> 00:04:08.873
Patient B has R of 0.066 and would present advanced.

00:04:08.873 --> 00:04:16.588
R ranks A first, but EAL is 0.20 for B against 0.072 for
A, nearly three times higher.

00:04:16.588 --> 00:04:23.227
Within a tier, EAL shows who has the most to lose if
surveillance is slow.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:25.939
One engine, two domains.

00:04:25.939 --> 00:04:29.489
The same three indicator families feed E, V and rho.

00:04:29.489 --> 00:04:35.362
In disaster science, hazards, climate change and
urbanisation raise exposure; poverty,

00:04:35.362 --> 00:04:41.575
governance and pollution raise vulnerability; and risk-
management strategy, infrastructure,

00:04:41.575 --> 00:04:44.853
and health and water security supply resilience.

00:04:44.853 --> 00:04:50.998
In liver cancer, the size of the at-risk population
feeds exposure; reduced liver reserve,

00:04:50.998 --> 00:04:57.895
metabolic disease and fibrosis progression feed
vulnerability; and surveillance uptake, test quality,

00:04:57.895 --> 00:05:01.514
access to cure and care coordination feed resilience.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:08.904
That is RMTI: one bounded equation, four levers, five
fixed tiers, and two domains.

00:05:08.904 --> 00:05:15.871
Next, see it applied: liver cancer surveillance, and
flood and heat risk in African cities.

00:05:15.871 --> 00:05:16.637
Thank you.

00:05:16.637 --> 00:05:18.321
I'm Dr. Cesar Marolla.

00:05:18.321 --> 00:05:23.297
The code is open under Apache 2.0, the demo uses no
patient data,

00:05:23.297 --> 00:05:27.125
and nothing here is medical or engineering advice.
